## What this document is

This is a working demonstration of the reporting template I build for clients. Everything below is produced by a single `.qmd` file: the styling, the interactive figures, the map and the table. There is no manual assembly step, no copy-paste into Word, and no separate slide deck to keep in sync. Re-running the file with new data reproduces the entire document exactly as you see it.

The data used here are a small selection of large marine protected areas, with rounded areas and indicative centroids taken from public sources. They are here to exercise the template, not to make an analytical point.

## An interactive figure

Hover any point for details, drag to zoom, double-click to reset. The line traces the cumulative area brought under protection by these designations.

```{r chart, fig.height=5}
plot_ly() |>
  add_trace(
    data = mpa,
    x = ~year, y = ~cumulative_km2 / 1e6,
    type = "scatter", mode = "lines",
    line = list(color = pal_ocean[["deep"]], width = 2, shape = "hv"),
    hoverinfo = "skip", name = "Cumulative area",
    showlegend = FALSE
  ) |>
  add_trace(
    data = mpa,
    x = ~year, y = ~cumulative_km2 / 1e6,
    type = "scatter", mode = "markers",
    color = ~basin, colors = basin_cols,
    marker = list(
      size = ~sqrt(area_km2) / 45 + 8,
      line = list(color = "white", width = 1.5),
      opacity = 0.9
    ),
    text = ~paste0(
      "<b>", name, "</b><br>",
      country, " · designated ", year, "<br>",
      format(area_km2, big.mark = " "), " km²"
    ),
    hoverinfo = "text"
  ) |>
  layout(
    xaxis = list(title = "Year of designation", gridcolor = "#eef3f6", zeroline = FALSE),
    yaxis = list(title = "Cumulative area (million km²)", gridcolor = "#eef3f6", zeroline = FALSE),
    legend = list(orientation = "h", y = -0.18, title = list(text = "")),
    hoverlabel = list(bgcolor = "white", bordercolor = pal_ocean[["surface"]]),
    font = list(family = "Source Sans 3, sans-serif", color = "#1a2332"),
    paper_bgcolor = "rgba(0,0,0,0)",
    plot_bgcolor = "rgba(0,0,0,0)",
    margin = list(t = 20)
  ) |>
  config(displayModeBar = FALSE)
```

Marker size scales with the area of each site. Colour separates ocean basins, and clicking a basin in the legend removes it from the plot.

## A spatial view

The same sites on a Leaflet map. Circles are scaled by area, the layer control in the top right switches basemaps and filters basins, and each marker carries a popup.

```{r map}
mpa_pal <- colorFactor(unname(basin_cols), domain = names(basin_cols))

m <- leaflet(mpa, options = leafletOptions(worldCopyJump = TRUE)) |>
  addProviderTiles("CartoDB.Positron", group = "Light") |>
  addProviderTiles("Esri.OceanBasemap", group = "Bathymetry") |>
  setView(lng = -160, lat = -15, zoom = 2)

for (b in names(basin_cols)) {
  d <- filter(mpa, basin == b)
  if (nrow(d) == 0) next
  m <- addCircleMarkers(
    m, data = d, lng = ~lon, lat = ~lat,
    radius = ~sqrt(area_km2) / 120 + 5,
    color = "white", weight = 1.5,
    fillColor = basin_cols[[b]], fillOpacity = 0.65,
    group = b,
    label = ~name,
    popup = ~paste0(
      "<div style='font-family:Source Sans 3,sans-serif;'>",
      "<b style='color:", pal_ocean[["deep"]], ";'>", name, "</b><br>",
      country, "<br>Designated ", year, "<br>",
      "<b>", format(area_km2, big.mark = " "), " km²</b></div>"
    )
  )
}

m |>
  addLayersControl(
    baseGroups = c("Light", "Bathymetry"),
    overlayGroups = names(basin_cols),
    options = layersControlOptions(collapsed = TRUE)
  ) |>
  addLegend(
    position = "bottomright", pal = mpa_pal, values = names(basin_cols),
    title = "Ocean basin", opacity = 0.8
  ) |>
  addScaleBar(position = "bottomleft")
```

Marker radius is illustrative rather than proportional to real footprint. In client work these become actual polygon boundaries, drawn from shapefiles or a spatial database.

## A sortable table

The same records as a searchable table. Sorting, filtering and pagination all work in the browser, so a reader can interrogate the underlying numbers without asking for the data file.

```{r table}
reactable(
  mpa |> select(name, country, basin, year, area_km2),
  columns = list(
    name     = colDef(name = "Site", minWidth = 200),
    country  = colDef(name = "Country or territory", minWidth = 140),
    basin    = colDef(name = "Basin", maxWidth = 110),
    year     = colDef(name = "Designated", maxWidth = 110, align = "center"),
    area_km2 = colDef(
      name = "Area (km²)", maxWidth = 130, align = "right",
      format = colFormat(separators = TRUE),
      style = function(value) {
        w <- value / max(mpa$area_km2)
        list(background = sprintf(
          "linear-gradient(90deg, %s%s %s%%, transparent %s%%)",
          pal_ocean[["light"]], "55", w * 100, w * 100
        ))
      }
    )
  ),
  searchable = TRUE, defaultPageSize = 8, highlight = TRUE,
  striped = TRUE, compact = TRUE,
  defaultSorted = list(area_km2 = "desc"),
  theme = reactableTheme(
    borderColor = "#e3ecf2",
    headerStyle = list(
      background = pal_ocean[["foam"]],
      color = pal_ocean[["deep"]],
      fontWeight = 600
    )
  )
)
```

## Why it is built this way

Three things matter to the clients I build these for. The document is **reproducible**, so a new data delivery means re-rendering rather than rebuilding. It is **self-contained**, a single HTML file that opens in any browser with no server, no login and no broken links a year later. And it is **readable by non-specialists**, because a decision-maker who can hover a point and read a popup will engage with results that a static PDF appendix would never get from them.

The template itself is CSS plus two HTML fragments referenced from the YAML header. Swapping the colour tokens and the footer produces a different institutional identity without touching any analysis code.

